## Why This Is Asked
Tests weighted interval scheduling with an exact k constraint and reconstruction; requires sorting, precomputing p(i), and a 2D DP with backtracking; also considerations for memory optimization.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling
- DP with an extra dimension for exact k selections
- Precomputing p(i) (latest non-overlapping job)
- Space-time tradeoffs; backtracking reconstruction
- Stability under large n and k; edge-case handling when K > possible non-overlapping sets

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonOverlapping(jobs, K) {
  // jobs: {s, e, r}; sort by end time
  jobs.sort((a,b)=> a.e - b.e);
  const n = jobs.length;
  const end = jobs.map(j=> j.e);
  // p(i): last index < i with end[p] < start[i]
  const p = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    let lo=0, hi=i-1, ans=-1;
    while (lo<=hi){
      const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
      if (end[mid] < jobs[i].s){ ans=mid; lo=mid+1; } else hi=mid-1;
    }
    p[i]=ans;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=1; t<=K; t++){
      const without = dp[i-1][t];
      const withTake = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? dp[p[i-1]+1][t-1] : (t-1===0?0: -Infinity)) + jobs[i-1].r;
      if (withTake > without){ dp[i][t] = withTake; take[i][t] = true; } else { dp[i][t] = without; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=K;
  while (i>0 && t>0){
    if (take[i][t]){ res.push(i-1); i = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? p[i-1]+1 : 0); t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return { revenue: dp[n][K], indices: res };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to an "at most K" constraint?
- How can you optimize memory to O(n + K) or O(K) and still reconstruct the solution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a concrete DP state definition on strings with reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] for substring ending at i with t flips
- Transitions handle 1 vs 0 at i; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space: O(nk) both; can optimize space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesWithFlips(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const start = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t] : i);
      } else if (t > 0) {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t-0 - 1] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t-1] : i);
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = 0;
        start[i][t] = -1;
      }
    }
  }
  // find max and reconstruct
  return {len: 0, l:0, r:0};
}
```

